You can join them by going to ScoutmasterCGcom and following the support or be a patron links at the top of the page.

3
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,260
And now to you, Scoutmaster,

4
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,160
Here's a little known fact from history.

5
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:33,380
Did you know that King Richard III of England ran a camping equipment store?

6
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:36,860
Yeah, you would have heard about it in Shakespeare.

7
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:41,900
There was a sign in the window of King Richard's camping store and it read:

8
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,260
Now is the winter of our discount tents.

9
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:49,760
Oh boy, Wow,

10
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,680
All right, if you know a better one, send it to me.

11
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,260
Hey, this is podcast number 36..

12
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:57,000
Discount:

13
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:23,420
Hey, this is Clark Green.

14
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,500
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

15
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:32,280
Now, just so, I mean, I hope everybody gets the joke at the beginning of the podcast this time:

16
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:34,740
Discount tent:

17
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,740
Now is the winter of our discount tents,

18
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,660
You know. and there's the famous Shakespeare line:

19
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:41,260
Now is the winter of our discontent.

20
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:47,700
Got a lot of emails on one, that, the one with the penguins paddling through the Sahara Desert.

21
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:49,940
I'm just trying to save a little time,

22
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:52,480
And time is at a premium this week.

23
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,780
Lots of things going on Scout-wise and other,

24
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:58,280
And I apologize,

25
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:07,500
I don't have the time to acknowledge all the emails and comments and various messages through the internets that I have been receiving,

26
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,340
But I will get around to them

27
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:13,520
And you know, do be in touch

28
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:15,160
And send me better jokes,

29
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,780
Because, wow,

30
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:17,800
I mean really,

31
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:22,100
We do have a few things to talk about this time around

32
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:27,880
In Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less, which is false advertising if there ever was,

33
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:32,100
Especially this week, because I think it ran in more, like 15 minutes.

34
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:34,300
But you understand, don't you?

35
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:35,420
Well, thank you,

36
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,720
I need a little help every once in a while,

37
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:43,760
So we're going to continue with the series about youth leadership and the patrol method.

38
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:48,620
And then I have a brief recommendation for a book that you might like to read.

39
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:49,840
Yeah, a book.

40
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,140
Remember those with the pages and everything.

41
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:53,360
They still make them.

42
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:54,400
Yeah,

43
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:58,840
And that'll be about it this time around.

44
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:06,660
You know, I do want to say thank you to several people who have mentioned that they're appreciative of the time that it takes to make the podcast.

45
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:08,520
It does take a lot of time

46
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:12,540
And it's time that I really don't have this time around,

47
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:17,280
But I sure do appreciate the thought every week

48
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,420
And, yeah, it does take a lot of time,

49
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:20,220
It's a lot of fun.

50
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,060
Well, sometimes it's a lot of fun,

51
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:27,460
But no, I appreciate the sentiment very much.

52
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:29,240
I'm glad that you're finding it useful.

53
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:32,840
And, speaking of useful, let's move on.

54
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:33,600
Yeah,

55
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:35,180
Let's get started, shall we?

56
00:03:35,180 --> 00:03:37,980
Scoutmastership in seven minutes

57
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:39,840
Or less.

58
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:57,180
This is the third in a series of talking about building a strong tradition of youth leadership within your troop and a strong patrol system,

59
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:58,760
And they're one in the same.

60
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,580
You know, both of those things come together.

61
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:07,300
Once you have good, strong youth leadership, you're going to see that the patrol system is going to start to grow.

62
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:19,640
Well, the first time we got together about this, we talked about reeducating ourselves and our fellow adult leaders as to what we're aiming at and what we really need to do,

63
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:32,380
And that is to get our troop on a solid ground of youth leadership and also to exercise a really robust, functioning patrol system.

64
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:36,640
So that was the first talk.

65
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:40,560
The second time we were together, we gave an example.

66
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:44,980
We kind of ran through a troop meeting and an outing and gave an example of what this looks like,

67
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,760
Just to get a picture in your head about what this looks like.

68
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:54,580
We talked about how this is going to be kind of messy and difficult, but we want it that way.

69
00:04:55,240 --> 00:05:04,380
And then, now that we're convinced, and now we have a picture in our mind of what this looks like, we're going to talk about how to work with your scouts to make this happen.

70
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:11,840
And the first kind of simple answer is: how are you going to make your scouts buy into this?

71
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:13,360
How are you going to have it happen?

72
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:14,420
Well, you're going to train them,

73
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:21,180
And our adult role in scouting is mostly training and mentoring our scouts,

74
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:24,960
And it's the most exciting and rewarding part of our job.

75
00:05:25,320 --> 00:05:26,300
It really is.

76
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:31,780
Now we've grown accustomed to looking at training as an event.

77
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:33,539
We have a training weekend,

78
00:05:33,679 --> 00:05:35,179
We have a training evening,

79
00:05:35,619 --> 00:05:42,280
We send the scouts off to a council training event or a district training event or even a national training event,

80
00:05:43,119 --> 00:05:46,500
And I think that the material shared in these- I think it's great.

81
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,340
But I question as to whether these things work really well.

82
00:05:50,340 --> 00:06:01,880
I've used the patrol leaders training thing and the troop leader training deal and gone through these as kind of events and programs

83
00:06:02,179 --> 00:06:06,200
And I find that we get not so great returns.

84
00:06:06,979 --> 00:06:08,280
Here's the reason why:

85
00:06:08,659 --> 00:06:15,219
Let's take your average scout, okay, and sit him down and teach him how to tie 20 knots,

86
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:18,340
20 knots.

87
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:21,919
So here's his chair and here's my chair and we're sitting down.

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,580
He has a piece of rope and I have a piece of rope, and then we start.

89
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,500
Well, by the third knot that poor scout is trying to find a way to escape,

90
00:06:30,979 --> 00:06:35,580
I mean, unless he's really interested in knots and he's really excited about it.

91
00:06:35,659 --> 00:06:42,500
But by the third knot or so he's starting to look at us like we're kind of torturing him and he's trying to find a way out.

92
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:49,320
By the fifth knot he's already figured that you're really going to try to teach him 20 knots,

93
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:50,500
And so he's kind of defeated

94
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:53,980
And he'll sit there and he'll go through the motions,

95
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:56,440
But he's resigned to sitting there and looking interested.

96
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:03,340
By knot number 10, he'll be able to tell you exactly how many ceiling tiles are in the room that you're sitting in.

97
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:05,419
But he's still trying, man,

98
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:06,619
He's trying to pay attention,

99
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:07,859
He's trying to work with you.

100
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:15,659
By knot number 18, he's going to be a little re-energized because now he sees the light at the end of the tunnel and he's going to get out.

101
00:07:15,799 --> 00:07:20,960
And at knot number 20, he'll bolt for the light and avoid you like the plague for the rest of his born days.

102
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:28,260
If you go back to that scout in a month's time and you sit down and you say, hey, I'd like you to show me the 20 knots I taught you.

103
00:07:28,659 --> 00:07:37,000
Well, after his horrified look, he'll maybe remember the name of five or six of them and he might remember how to tie two or three of them,

104
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:40,080
But the others are just kind of a bad dream.

105
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:47,120
Compare that with showing him how to tie a knot he needs at the very moment.

106
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:50,100
So he's got to put up a tarp,

107
00:07:50,580 --> 00:07:51,420
We're out camping.

108
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:52,460
He's got to put up a tarp

109
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:54,380
And he's got the line in his hand.

110
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:56,440
He's got the tarp

111
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:57,880
And you stop through

112
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:00,040
And in a moment you show him a taut line hitch

113
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:00,920
And he's very appreciative.

114
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:02,720
He says, oh, that's really kind of cool.

115
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:03,640
Show me how to do that.

116
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:04,820
And you tie the knot

117
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:07,620
And you let him practice it a couple of times

118
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:08,880
And there he is.

119
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:17,100
And so long as we resist the temptation of telling him the history of the taut line hitch and how the particular rope he's using was made, and going on and on and on,

120
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:21,020
You know he's going to get something from that experience.

121
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:26,680
So my contention is is that scouts will internalize things they find immediately practical.

122
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:28,520
It will become a part of them.

123
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:34,539
When learning and training is divorced from practicality, they're much less interested.

124
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:36,100
And who could blame them?

125
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:37,679
I mean, really,

126
00:08:37,679 --> 00:08:50,739
Why would we want to give away the most interesting, engaging and really gratifying part of our job as leaders to some kind of program or some kind of event?

127
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:51,699
No,

128
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:59,000
Our most gratifying work is done by developing leadership, by mentoring it,

129
00:08:59,199 --> 00:09:00,420
And it's not an event,

130
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:02,500
It's not a training event.

131
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:11,600
It is an ongoing process that will start when a boy enters the troop and will end when he ages out.

132
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:14,020
This is an important thing to think about.

133
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:17,960
Look at this training and mentoring as a process.

134
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:27,060
Interestingly enough, I got a great email from someone who asked the exact questions that we are addressing right now.

135
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:36,020
So you know I have. basically, his question was: my scouts aren't really motivated to lead.

136
00:09:36,819 --> 00:09:42,359
We go camping and they see other people, like you know, hiking the Appalachian Trail and doing stuff.

137
00:09:42,439 --> 00:09:45,979
They see other scout troops doing this and they say, yeah, we'd kind of like to do that,

138
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:53,239
But then, when the rubber meets the road, they kind of sit around their patrol leader's council table and stare at each other and they don't really know what to do.

139
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:59,600
I'm going to say that they are motivated to lead because they see what they would like to do from afar

140
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:02,740
And they have a model of what they'd like to do.

141
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:08,640
I mean, you see these other guys out on a backpacking trip or something like that, while they're just sitting around playing cards,

142
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:12,340
And so they have a goal.

143
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:14,600
They don't know it, but they have a goal.

144
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:22,360
Now, once we've gotten ourselves out of the way and we're no longer planning and doing and presenting a program for the scouts,

145
00:10:23,220 --> 00:10:32,080
and when they have some kind of a goal or some kind of a vision, they will begin to respond.

146
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:36,340
And what we have to do first of all is spend a little time putting ourselves in their shoes.

147
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:43,900
I mean, do they have any frame of reference for active, getting things done kind of a troop that we have as our goal?

148
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:46,860
Do they understand anything about making things happen?

149
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,700
I've got to tell you, most scout age boys don't have this frame of reference.

150
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:57,300
They tend to be a bit passive as far as leadership goes, a bit reluctant to step into any kind of leadership role,

151
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,440
and they're always willing to follow the path of least resistance.

152
00:11:01,740 --> 00:11:03,440
I mean, I can remember being that way,

153
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:03,800
Can you?

154
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:11,140
I mean, and often our frustration lies in that we've told them what to do, we've sent them to training events

155
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:14,500
and they still seem to be relatively clueless.

156
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:17,819
I mean, they just don't pick up the ball and run with it.

157
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:24,400
They need some kind of response to any promising initiative they may show

158
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:30,819
and something to catalyze their interest and to catalyze their leadership skills.

159
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:40,740
Our job, in way of supporting their efforts, is to find that little spark of initiative and fan it with lots of encouragement and direction.

160
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:45,220
Find that asking questions is a pretty good way to catalyze initiative.

161
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:50,160
These are questions they don't ask themselves simply because they don't know how to ask them.

162
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:56,500
And when we ask questions, instead of telling scouts what to do, we're helping them discover answers.

163
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:04,839
So, using the example: okay, here's some guys and they see this other troop going off on a backpacking trip and they think, wow, that would be great to do.

164
00:12:05,339 --> 00:12:06,560
You can ask them a question.

165
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:11,160
Well, how do you figure? the other guys put together this backpacking trip?

166
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:14,140
I don't know. I don't know how they did it.

167
00:12:14,719 --> 00:12:17,400
Well, do you want to go hiking right

168
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,160
Now? how would you make that happen?

169
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:21,900
Well, I don't know,

170
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:26,480
Maybe we'd need to get a map or something.

171
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:29,120
Okay, well, that'd be a good start.

172
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:30,880
Now, where do you figure you can find a map?

173
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:33,540
Well, I don't know,

174
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:35,140
I'm not really sure.

175
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:41,280
Well, what if I got you a map and we could sit down and look at it and plan a hiking trip?

176
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:45,740
So we start with that little spark of initiative and we try to catalyze it,

177
00:12:45,860 --> 00:12:50,439
We try and bring it into flame just by asking some questions and offering some assistance.

178
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:57,420
So we get the map and we've got a bit going on there, and then we have a planning session,

179
00:12:57,819 --> 00:13:01,540
A real simple planning session that lasts for a few minutes,

180
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,100
And I mean a few minutes.

181
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,660
We don't want to drown them in the 17 steps to planning a hiking trip.

182
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:14,120
We don't want to get out a PowerPoint presentation and a syllabus and start, you know, beating them over the head with it.

183
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:19,380
We want to move them along by very small degrees and help them discover what to do next.

184
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:29,180
All right now, at this stage of things, we're doing more leading and we're doing more directing than we ultimately want to when our youth leaders take over in the future.

185
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:33,260
But this is all about assuring the success of their first efforts.

186
00:13:33,739 --> 00:13:38,060
We're absolutely taking them by the hand and leading them step by step,

187
00:13:38,239 --> 00:13:44,339
We're giving them directions and we're doing a lot more leading than we would ultimately like to do.

188
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:53,160
But if we give them a frame of reference for the job of leadership and show them what it looks like, they're going to accept it and they're going to figure it out.

189
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:54,560
Think about learning to ride a bike.

190
00:13:54,640 --> 00:14:00,200
You can't teach someone how to ride a bike with diagrams and classroom instruction.

191
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:02,840
You have to put their tail in the seat.

192
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:08,540
Then you walk along with them and talk them through the idea of balancing and you remind them to pedal,

193
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:09,840
You show them how the brakes work.

194
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:17,520
Soon you're, you know, running along with a hand on the bike and soon thereafter you let go. but you're still kind of running along beside them

195
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,580
And soon thereafter they're off riding on their own and we step back and let them at it.

196
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:25,400
So that's the kind of process we're talking about:

197
00:14:25,540 --> 00:14:27,340
Degrees of development,

198
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:31,240
Degrees of gaining some expertise in leadership.

199
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:34,440
You know this is a delicate process.

200
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:42,020
Scout age boys are often extremely sensitive to criticism from us and from their peers.

201
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:43,600
They get wounded pretty easily.

202
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:48,760
They are full of bravado that masks a real basic uncertainty that a lot of them have.

203
00:14:48,900 --> 00:14:51,640
They add a lot of reassurance and encouragement

204
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:58,400
And that's why it's vital that we put ourselves in their shoes and do our best to see the world from their perspective.

205
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,360
The first successes in this are going to be small.

206
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:02,980
They're on a boy's scale.

207
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:08,380
Small successes are going to build into larger ones and then things get rolling.

208
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:14,740
Once a group of scouts have shared in some successful attempts at making things happen,

209
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:18,400
they'll develop the confidence and the skills and the vision to carry on.

210
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:23,360
And then they're going to start passing them on to the younger scouts,

211
00:15:24,280 --> 00:15:33,939
So catalyzing their very small, you know, perhaps very dim, perhaps almost non-existent initiative.

212
00:15:35,140 --> 00:15:42,800
And, like I say, my favorite way to do that is just to ask some questions and then build on very small successes,

213
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:55,199
Build on, you know, barely positive answers and kind of guide them into this idea of becoming a leader, of having some vision, having some skills.

214
00:15:55,200 --> 00:16:04,020
And this is, as I said at the outset, one of the most gratifying things we get to do as scout leaders.

215
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:07,440
Now, these are broad, underlying concepts.

216
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:10,300
Again, hey, it's our favorite subject,

217
00:16:10,500 --> 00:16:13,640
It's our favorite stock and trade here at the Scoutmaster podcast.

218
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:19,740
You know these are things that we have to think about and consider over and, over and over again.

219
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:30,720
So here's, you know, the four-step program to getting your troop on a solid tradition of youth leadership.

220
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:39,180
One is to understand that this is an education that is happening for adults more than scouts.

221
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:42,420
It's happening for adults more than scouts.

222
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:49,839
We have to rethink things and we have to make sure our adult leaders are on board and that we're all sharing this goal.

223
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:53,560
Then we need to understand what this is going to look like.

224
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:56,860
We need to have some picture in our mind of what this is going to look like-

225
00:16:57,660 --> 00:17:00,260
And we went through that the last time we were together-

226
00:17:01,079 --> 00:17:05,899
And then we want to start building on very small victories.

227
00:17:06,579 --> 00:17:09,740
Then we need to look at this training as an ongoing process.

228
00:17:09,879 --> 00:17:10,800
It's never going to stop.

229
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:16,619
You're going to be, you know, working with guys at different levels of development all the time,

230
00:17:16,619 --> 00:17:29,760
But once you've had a good, solid year or so of you know, a good, solid tradition of youth leadership within your troop, it'll begin to perpetuate itself.

231
00:17:30,340 --> 00:17:35,100
It's mostly work on our conceptions and the way that we approach things,

232
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:36,580
But it does work.

233
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:38,120
It really does work

234
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:40,560
And I really encourage you to do it

235
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:43,100
Well. I'd love to answer any questions you have around this,

236
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:44,000
Hear your stories,

237
00:17:44,580 --> 00:17:46,040
Listen to your suggestions.

238
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:54,120
Email me at ClarkGreen C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmailcom.

239
00:17:54,120 --> 00:18:00,179
The sun goes down and with it goes the light.

240
00:18:00,180 --> 00:18:04,900
So I fire up my lantern and put my jacket and thermals on.

241
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,760
They keep me from freezing.

242
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:10,280
In a minute I'll be warm inside my sleeping bag.

243
00:18:10,660 --> 00:18:13,680
There I'll hide until the light of dawn has come.

244
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:18,580
But here there's no TV, no hum of machines,

245
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:22,920
No sitting too close to computer screens.

246
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:27,400
No, I've got a flashlight and by that I can read

247
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:29,380
And you hear that running water.

248
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,400
Well, that's all that I need.

249
00:18:32,360 --> 00:18:35,360
Yeah, every day is like camping for me.

250
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:37,640
Since they shut off my power.

251
00:18:37,640 --> 00:19:01,580
The life of George W Sears is now more than a century away from us.

252
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:03,120
He was born in 1821.

253
00:19:04,300 --> 00:19:06,580
And he lived until 1890..

254
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:13,360
George was a sports writer for Forest and Stream magazine in the 1880s and an early conservationist.

255
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:24,220
He was one of the first people to write about camping and how to go camping and what to do and what not to do,

256
00:19:24,939 --> 00:19:28,360
And his pen name is Nesmuk N-E-S-S-M-U-K.

257
00:19:28,899 --> 00:19:34,620
He was the eldest of ten children and he was born in Webster, Massachusetts.

258
00:19:34,620 --> 00:19:40,159
He took his pen name from an American Indian who had befriended him in childhood

259
00:19:41,060 --> 00:19:44,239
And he was always fascinated with American Indian culture

260
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:48,219
And he had an abiding interest in forest life and adventure.

261
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:53,199
He was a factory laborer while he was still a child

262
00:19:53,200 --> 00:20:00,300
And he, at age 12, started working in the commercial fishing fleet out of Cape Cod, Massachusetts.

263
00:20:00,740 --> 00:20:05,400
At 19, he signed on for a three-year voyage on a whaler, headed for the South Pacific.

264
00:20:05,400 --> 00:20:15,840
It was the same year- actually 1841, that Herman Melville shipped out of the same port, bound for the same whaling grounds.

265
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:17,440
You remember Herman, don't you?

266
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:23,540
He ended up moving to Wellsboro, Pennsylvania, where he was to live for the rest of his life,

267
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:34,260
And he continued traveling for adventure in the upper Midwest and Ontario and even down to the Amazon in Brazil.

268
00:20:34,820 --> 00:20:38,399
He wrote a book that is still in print today.

269
00:20:38,639 --> 00:20:45,680
There's not a whole lot of books from the 1880s that are still in print today and are still being sold,

270
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:47,940
But it's one I recommend to you heartily.

271
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:50,440
It's called Woodcraft and Camping

272
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:53,860
And it is a great read still

273
00:20:54,380 --> 00:21:00,700
Now. of course, some of his equipment, ideas and methods and things like that are outdated,

274
00:21:01,420 --> 00:21:08,500
But his philosophy on approaching the outdoors is just as solid as the day that he wrote it.

275
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:13,880
There's a quote from him in Woodcraft and Camping that I think is one that I read about 20 years ago

276
00:21:13,880 --> 00:21:19,080
And I've just about committed it to memory and I refer to it every once in a while

277
00:21:19,460 --> 00:21:23,480
He says: we do not go to the green woods and crystal waters to rough it,

278
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:25,640
We go to smooth it.

279
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:28,220
You know, we get it rough enough at home.

280
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:35,260
Camping isn't about roughing it. in his eyes,

281
00:21:35,260 --> 00:21:45,160
It's about escaping from society and getting off into the woods and getting back into touch with something that's at the basis of our hearts and souls.

282
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:50,940
I think you'll enjoy reading George W Sears and Woodcraft and Camping.

283
00:21:51,140 --> 00:21:54,140
Now. it's easily available on a PDF version

284
00:21:54,940 --> 00:21:59,300
And I'll have a link to that on the post for podcast number 36..

285
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:00,840
But I'll tell you what.

286
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,100
Why don't you do this instead?

287
00:22:02,100 --> 00:22:08,620
Why don't you just get a good, old-fashioned copy of the actual book that you can hold in your hands?

288
00:22:09,020 --> 00:22:10,040
It's a small book.

289
00:22:10,639 --> 00:22:13,300
It doesn't take too long to read.

290
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:17,580
I imagine anybody could sit down and read it in an hour and an hour and a half.

291
00:22:18,159 --> 00:22:19,419
I think it'll give you a chuckle,

292
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:21,219
It'll make you stop and think,

293
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:25,240
And there are still some pretty good ideas about camping in there.

